<!--
  `F-112` — the launcher icon, second pass.

  ## What changed and why

  The first version put the viewfinder brackets around the slash. At 48 dp on a
  home screen those four corner strokes collapsed into visual noise around a
  small mark - detail that survives on a 512 px store listing and dies at the
  size the icon is actually seen. Gone.

  What is left is the slash alone, on gold, in black. Reversed from the wordmark
  on purpose: an app icon sits on a wallpaper nobody controls, so it has to
  carry its own contrast. Gold on black relies on the launcher's backdrop being
  dark; black on gold does not rely on anything.

  Thick and tall for the same reason. The stroke is 20 units of a 108 viewport
  and runs nearly the full height of the safe zone, which is what keeps it
  legible at 36 dp in a folder and in a 1-bit monochrome themed icon.

  Adaptive geometry: 108 x 108 viewport, safe zone 18..90 on both axes. Every
  point below is inside it, so no launcher mask - circle, squircle or rounded
  square - can clip the mark.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">

    <!-- The slash. SWIP Ink on gold. -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#060507"
        android:pathData="M56,21 L76,21 L52,87 L32,87 Z" />
</vector>
